# Facial Nerve Palsy

- **Anatomy of the facal nerve:**
  - <u>Physiological function of facial nerve</u> - a mixed nerve:
    - Motor supply to the facial muscles
    - Somatic afferents to EAC and pinna
    - Afferent fibres from tast receptors in the anterior 2/3 of the tongue
    - Parasympathetic fibres innervating lacrimal glands, sabmandibular and sublingual glands
  - **Course of the facial nerve** - fibres in the facial nuclei emerges from the ventrolateral pons, and course of facial nerve can be divided into 3 distinct anatomical regions:
    - **Intracranial segment** (~ 10mm) - motor root and nervus intermedius (visceral mixed root) emerges from the pontomedullary junction laterally through the CPA together w/ CN VIII, and enters the <u>internal acoustic meatus</u>
    - **Fallopian segment** (~ 33mm) - extends through the internal acoustic meatus through the stylomastoid foramen, with a labyrinthine, tympanic and mastoid component, giving off 3 key branches:
      - <u>Greater petrosal nerve</u> - branch from the geniculate ganglion, entering the middle cranial fossa extradurally, and exits through the foramen lacerum towards the pterygopalatine ganglion to supply the lacrimal glands
      - <u>Nerve to stapedius</u> - passes to innervate stapedius muscle in the tympanic cavity of the middle ear
      - <u>Chorda tympani</u> - parasympathetic fibres and special sensory fibers for taste
    - **Extracranial component** - descends through the stylomastoid foramen and divides to 5 branches between the deep and superficial lobes of the parotid gland at the pes anserinus ('goose foot'), innervating muscles of facial expression in variable pattern:
      - <u>Frontal branch</u> - motor innervation to the frontalis muscle
      - <u>Zygomatic branch</u> - motor innervation to the orbicularis oculi muscle
      - <u>Buccal branch</u> - motor innervation to the buccinator muscle
      - <u>Marginal mandibular branch</u> - motor innervation to levator anguli orbis
      - <u>Cervical branch</u> - motor innervation to the platysma
- **DDx of ipsilateral facial nerve palsy:**
  - **Intra-cranial disease:**
    - <u>Supranuclear</u> (UMN facial nerve palsy) - CNS tumours or stroke
    - <u>Lesions of the facial nucleus</u> - pontine lesions:
      - Vascular - stroke
      - Neoplastic - tumours within the pons
      - Inflammatory - multiple sclerosis
      - Degenerative - syringobulbia (may be bilateral)
    - <u>Lesions in the posterior cranial fossa</u> - acoustic schwannoma, leptomeningeal disease (e.g. meningioma, chronic meningitis)
  - **Temporal bone disease:**
    - Traumatic - temporal bone fracture
    - Infective/ idiopathic - Bell's palsy, Ramsey Hunt syndrome, Otitis Media (acute or a/w cholesteatoma)
    - Neoplastic - glomus, carcinoma of the middle ear
  - **Parotid gland disease:**
    - Neoplastic - malignant tumour in the parotid gland, metastatic intraparotid LN
    - Iatrogenic - surgical injury
- **DDx of bilateral facial weakness:**
  - Bilateral parotid disease - e.g. Warthin's tumour. Acinic cell carcinoma
  - Peripheral neuropathies - e.g. Guillain-Barre syndrome, Lyme disease, mononeuritis multiplex
  - Disease of the muscles or NMJ - e.g. botulism, myasthenia gravis, myopathies
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - site, onset, progression, quality, severity, timing:
    - <u>Site</u> - ipsilateral vs bilateral
    - <u>Onset</u> - acute vs chronic, sudden vs gradual:
      - **Acute onset** - must r/o life-threatening diseases such as stroke
      - **Chronic onset** - points to other causes rather than a cerebrovascular incident
    - <u>How did the patient first notice facial weakness?</u>
      - Visible facial asymmetry - in the mirror vs family and friends noticed the facial asymetry
      - Dysarthria and difficulty in keeping liquids in mouth
    - <u>Progression</u> - gradual progressive vs variable course:
      - **Gradual progression** - points towards neoplastic conditions
      - **Variable course** - suggestive of disease of NMJ (esp. with Hx of fatiguability)
    - <u>Timing</u>:
      - **Worse at night** - may be fatiguability due to MG
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - <u>Associated neurological Sx</u> - esp. in acute setting:
      - **Motor deficits:**
        - Ipsilateral limb weakness - suggestive of cortical lesion (vascular vs neoplastic)
        - Contralateral limb weakness - suggestive of pontine lesion
      - **Hemisensory loss:**
        - Ipsilateral hemisensory loss - suggestive of cortical lesion, but if limb-sparing, maybe due to a CPA lesion resulting in ipsilateral CN V palsy
      - **Diplopia** - MG or myopathies
      - **Bulbar Sx** (Dysphagia and dysarthria) - can mimic facial weakness
    - <u>Associated Sx of facial nerve palsy</u>:
      - **Dry eyes and mouth** - due to decreased lacrimation and salivary production points towards a lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion or involves salivatory nucleuses
      - **Hyperacusis** - suggests stapedius paralysis due to involvement of the nerve to stapedius, likely caused by a lesion in CPA (lacrimation lost) or facial canal (lacrimation spared)
      - **Loss of taste** - chorda tympani involvement suggestive of lesion in the facial canal or potential CPA
    - <u>Associated Otological Sx</u>:
      - **Otalgia** - suggestive of inflammatory process within the middle ear
      - **Ipsilateral hearing loss** - suggestive of ipsilateral ear pathology
    - <u>Parotid swelling</u> - ascertain ipsilateral and bilateral and associated features
- **P/E of facial nerve palsy:**
  - **Principles of facial nerve examination:**
    - Examination of motor function - test 5 branches of facial nerve in a systematic manner
    - Examination of external and middle ear - look for vesicles that could be explained by Ramsey Hunt Syndrome
    - Examination of tha parotid glands and neck
  - **Examination of motor functions of facial nerve:**
    - <u>Inspection</u> - look for resting signs if facial nerve palsy:
      - Frontal branch - loss of wrinkling over the forehead
      - Zygomatic branch - ectropion
      - Buccal branch - deviation of the nasal tip and loss of nasolabial folds
      - Marginal mandibular branch - drooping of mouth or drooling
    - <u>Testing for muscle power</u>:
      - Frontal branch - ask patient to raise eyebrows
      - Zygomatic branch - ask patient to shut both eyes and resist examiner from opening them
      - Buccal branch - ask patient to puff out cheeks
      - Marginal mandibular branch - ask patient to show teeth and smile
  - **Examination of the external and middle ear** - vesicles and or scabbing in the EAC suggestive of Ramsey Hunt syndrome due to Zoster Reactivation
  - **Examination of the parotid glands** - inspect for mass lesions within the parotid gland
- **Ix** - depends on the suspected segment affected:
  - **Imaging:**
    - <u>CT/ BRI brain</u> - for suspected intracranial lesion
    - <u>MRI/CT temporal bone</u> - for suspected middle ear pathology or temporal bone fracture
    - <u>MRI/ CT parotid and USG FNA</u> - if parotid gland disease suspected
  - **Electrophysiological testing** - guides prognosis and necessity of operative decompression:
    - <u>Electromyography</u> - determine activity of muscle itself
    - <u>Electroneurography</u> (ENoG) - guides prognosis for spontaneous recovery and the necessity of surgical decompression

## Bell's Palsy

- **Definition** - idiopathic facial nerve palsy constituting most common cause of peripheral facial nerve palsy postulated to be caused by swelling of the facial nerve within the facial canal (esp. Labyrinthine segment)
- **Epidemiology** - accounts for 50% of peripheral facial nerve palsy:
  - <u>Incidence</u> - 12-50/100000/y
  - <u>Demographic</u> - no agem sex or geographic predilection
- **Pathophysiology of Bell's Palsy** - not completely understood:
  - **Herpes simplex reactivation** - postulated HSV reactivation along the facial nerve resulting in inflammation is the most widely accepted cause of Bell's palsy, but evidence is inconclusive (some role of antivirals postulated)
  - **Inflammation of the facial nerve** - oedematous and inflammatory changes in the facial nerve (esp. perineurium) results in entrapment of the facial nerve within the tight facial canal, which is <u>maximal in the narrow labyrinthine compartment</u>
- **Clinical features of Bell's palsy:**
  - **Ipsilateral facial weakness** - dominant S usually sudden onset of ipsilateral complete facial weakness that becomes maximal over hours:
    - <u>Site</u> - weakness or paralysis of both the upper and lower portions of the face
    - <u>Onset</u> - sudden onset over hours
    - <u>Progression</u> - onset over several hours, but may continue to worsen over days, up to 3 weeks, but subsequently stabilises and spontaneously improve (failure of recovery, esp. with Tx raises suspicion of alternative diagnosis)
  - **Hyperacusis** - sudden onset of hyperacusis as motor innervation to stapedius is impaired
  - **Decreased lacrimation and salivation** - notable Sx if lesion proximal or at the geniculate ganglion
  - **Loss of taste** - usually noticeable suggestive of Chorda Tympani involvement
- **Dx evaluation of Bell's palsy** - a clinical diagnosis if presentation is typical:
  - **Features suggestive of Bell's palsy:**
    - <u>Typical pattern of facial weakness</u> - ipsilateral upper and lower facial weakness w/ associated loss of taste lacrimation or hyperacusis
    - <u>Typical temporal pattern</u> - acute onset (within hours) and reaches maximal intensity with 3 weeks with resolution spontaneously or with treatment
  - **Features atypical Bell's palsy:**
    - <u>Atypical pattern of facial weakness</u> - ipsilateral facial weakness w/ upper face spared, or bilateral acute facial weakness
    - <u>Additional neurological S/S</u> - cranial neuropathies (e.g. CN V, VIII), signs of meningeal irritation
    - <u>Additional systemic features</u> - e.g. fever
    - <u>Atypical temporal pattern</u> - insiduous onset, progressive worsening or no improvement w/ Tx
